    "passage": "Do you like to wear a sweatshirt? When you wear it, have you ever looked down and noticed a small triangle [[1]] right below the neck? And have you ever wondered why it’s there? Though a fashion statement today, this triangle, known as the V-notch, [[2]] some practical purposes when the sweatshirt was invented a century ago.\nIn the 1920s, Benjamin Russell Jr., a quarterback for a football team, was upset about the constant itching caused by the team’s wool football uniforms. Seeking a more comfortable [[3]], he partnered with his father, who owned the Russell Manufacturing Company. Together, they developed a thick cotton practice jersey that ensured both comfort and durability. Their [[4]] eventually led to the creation of a new division: the Russell Athletic Company, dedicated solely to sweatshirt production.\nThe V-notch has remained one [[5]] feature of the sweatshirt since its introduction by Russell Athletic in 1926. Though it may appear purely decorative today, the V-notch was originally a functional [[6]]. The design made it easier for the wearer to slip on the sweatshirt. As the wearer gets their head through the neck of the sweatshirt, the V-notch would stretch to [[7]]. Also, since the chest is a major point of perspiration, the thicker cotton panel of the V-notch could help absorb [[8]], keeping athletes dry during intense physical activity.\nOver time, the V-notch continues to [[9]] its presence though sweatshirts have evolved from athletic gear into everyday casual wear. It may appear [[10]] today, but its existence adds a touch of old-school charm to modern sweatshirts, giving each piece an elevated look. So next time you wear your favorite sweatshirt, you’ve got to appreciate this significant design.",
